2002
        The Jorgensen lab linux cluster consists of a mix
of Pentium-based PCs ranging from 333 MHz Pentium IIs to
2.4 GHz Pentium IVs, mostly dual-processor machines from Dell.              

The newest ones are configured as follows:                 
        2 - Intel 2.4 GHz Xeon processors
        1 GB RAM 
        80 GB SCSI hard drive
        100 baseT ethernet

        All of the machines are running linux 2.4.18, which is a very
stable and robust version of the freeware operating system, linux.
We obtained the kernel itself via ftp from the internet--linux is
freeware and is freely available; see http://www.linux.org. We have,
however, used the main installation and formatting utilities from
Caldera (http://www.caldera.com), and we have also used Red Hat
linux (http://www.redhat.com). Both companies have satisfactory
variants of linux, as well as instructions and support.
        The dual-processor machines use a version of the 2.2.7 kernel
that was compiled here in the lab. This mainly involves configuring the
kernel with various device drivers and then setting a flag that causes
the kernel to be compiled for SMP (shared memory multi-processing) and
then compiling the kernel. Caldera and Red Hat do not readily supply a
pre-compiled SMP kernel with the drivers compiled in the kernel, but
rather use loadable modules for most hardware devices, so it is
generally necessary to compile the SMP kernel oneself.
        Because of the computationally intensive nature of the simulations
that are performed in the lab, we have attempted to use the most reliable
and efficient FORTAN compilers possible for the generation of our software.
To that end, we have tested a variety of FORTRAN compilers, and the one that
we have determined to combine best ease of use with efficient compiled software 
is from Intel. A reasonable alternative
is from the Portland Group, Inc. (PGI); see http://www.pgroup.com.
        In order to efficiently use our mix of ca. 100 processors, the cluster
has a queuing system implemented. We have been using DQS 3.1 from
Florida State University; http://www.scri.fsu.edu. DQS, like linux, is
free software.

